export default {"id":"m4l-level-11","number":11,"title":"Module 11","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"11.1","modules":[{"id":"m4l-m-11-1-1","code":"11.1","title":"11.1","blurb":"11.1","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"m4l-l-11-1-1","title":"11.1.1: Audio Craft — Dynamics and Gain Processing","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Metadata and Prerequisites","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Lesson ID:</strong> Lesson 11.1.1","<strong>Module / Section:</strong> Module 11 / Section 11.1","<strong>Track:</strong> <code>m4l-devices</code>","<strong>Target Environment:</strong> Ableton Live 12 Standard (with Max for Live add-on) / Live 12 Suite; bundled Cycling '74 Max 9 runtime.","<strong>Operating Systems:</strong> Windows, macOS.","<strong>Prerequisites:</strong> Lesson 10.4.1 - Advanced MIDI — MPE Processing and Passthrough.","<strong>Primary Sources:</strong>","Cycling '74: <em>Max 9 MSP Guide: Dynamics, Envelope Following, and Peak Detection</em>.","Ableton Production Guidelines: <em>Audio Dynamics and Gain Architecture</em>.","Ableton Patch Code Standard: <em>Click-Free Parameter Smoothing</em>."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By the conclusion of this module, the student will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["Implement real-time audio envelope followers using <code>[average~]</code>, <code>[peakamp~]</code>, and <code>[rampsmooth~]</code>.","Construct a complete dynamic range compressor topology incorporating Threshold, Ratio, Attack, Release, and Makeup Gain controls.","Implement true stereo-linked sidechain envelope detection to prevent stereo image shifting during dynamic gain reduction.","Calculate gain reduction curves in decibels and convert to linear amplitude scalars using <code>[dbtoa]</code>.","Integrate visual Gain Reduction (GR) metering on the device front panel using <code>[live.meter~]</code>."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Architectural and Theoretical Foundations","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"3.1 Dynamics Processing Topology","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"A feedback/feedforward compressor consists of four interconnected mathematical stages:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                           COMPRESSOR SIGNAL TOPOLOGY                          |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                               |\n|  [ plugin~ ] (Stereo Audio In: Left &amp; Right)                                  |\n|       |                                                                       |\n|       +------------------------------------+                                  |\n|       | (Audio Signal Path)                | (Sidechain Detection Path)       |\n|       v                                    v                                  |\n|  [ Audio Delay Line ]             [ Stereo Link Detector ]                    |\n|  (Compensates attack time)        (Sums L + R / Peak Level)                   |\n|       |                                    |                                  |\n|       |                                    v                                  |\n|       |                           [ Envelope Ballistics ]                     |\n|       |                           (rampsmooth~ Attack / Release)              |\n|       |                                    |                                  |\n|       |                                    v                                  |\n|       |                           [ Threshold &amp; Ratio Math ]                  |\n|       |                           (Calculates Gain Reduction in dB)           |\n|       |                                    |                                  |\n|       |                                    v                                  |\n|       |                           [ Makeup Gain + dbtoa ]                     |\n|       |                                    |                                  |\n|       |                                    +------------------------------+   |\n|       |                                    | (Gain Reduction Signal)      |   |\n|       v                                    v                              v   |\n|  [ *~ VCA Multiplier ] &lt;===================+                      [ GR Meter ]|\n|       |                                                                       |\n|       v                                                                       |\n|  [ plugout~ ] (Processed Audio to Live)                                       |\n|                                                                               |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"heading","text":"3.2 Gain Reduction Mathematics","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"When the detected signal level L_in (dB) exceeds the user-defined Threshold T (dB), gain reduction GR (dB) is calculated according to the compression Ratio R:"},{"type":"prose","html":"GR (dB) = cases 0, &amp; if L_in T \\\\ ( 1 - 1R ) × (T - L_in), &amp; if L_in &gt; T cases"},{"type":"prose","html":"Adding Makeup Gain M (dB):"},{"type":"prose","html":"Total Gain (dB) = GR (dB) + M (dB)"},{"type":"prose","html":"Linear Scalar:"},{"type":"prose","html":"VCA = 10^ Total Gain20 (calculated via [dbtoa])"}]},{"type":"step","title":"4. Envelope Ballistics: [rampsmooth~]","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To prevent distortion caused by audio-rate envelope ripples (especially on low-frequency bass signals), the rectified audio signal must be smoothed using separate Attack and Release time constants:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong><code>[rampsmooth~ &lt;up_samples&gt; &lt;down_samples&gt;]</code>:</strong> Applies sample-accurate smoothing:","<code>up_samples</code>: Governs compressor Attack time (ramp up in signal level).","<code>down_samples</code>: Governs compressor Release time (decay back to baseline)."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                      [ Absolute Signal Level: abs~ ]\n                                     |\n                                     v\n                  +-------------------------------------+\n                  |   rampsmooth~ 480 4800 (Atk, Rel)   |\n                  +------------------+------------------+\n                                     |\n                                     v (Smoothed Envelope)</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"5. Laboratory Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Objective","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Construct a mastering-grade downward audio compressor with threshold, ratio, attack, release, makeup gain, and gain reduction metering."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step-by-Step Procedure","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["In Ableton Live 12, insert a <strong>Max Audio Effect</strong> on a drum track and select <strong>Edit in Max</strong>.","Enable <strong>Open in Presentation</strong> in the Patcher Inspector.","Stereo Ingestion: Ingest audio from <code>[plugin~]</code>.","Stereo Detection:","Sum Left and Right channels: <code>[+~]</code> → <code>[*~ 0.5]</code>.","Extract absolute level: <code>[abs~]</code>.","Convert to RMS envelope: <code>[rampsmooth~ 480 4800]</code>.","Threshold &amp; Compression Math:","Convert amplitude envelope to dB: <code>[atodb~]</code>.","Create <code>[live.dial Threshold]</code> (Float, <code>-60 to 0 dB</code>, default: <code>-12 dB</code>).","Create <code>[live.dial Ratio]</code> (Float, <code>1.0 to 20.0</code>, default: <code>4.0</code>).","Subtract Threshold from signal level. If positive, scale by (1 - 1/R).","Makeup Gain:","Create <code>[live.dial Makeup]</code> (Float, <code>0 to 24 dB</code>). Sum with gain reduction.","Convert back to linear scalar: <code>[dbtoa~]</code>.","Apply VCA:","Multiply Left and Right audio from <code>[plugin~]</code> by the gain reduction signal using <code>[*~]</code>.","Route to <code>[plugout~]</code>.","Gain Reduction Metering:","Route GR (dB) through <code>[snapshot~ 30]</code> → <code>[live.meter~]</code>.","Add all parameters to Presentation Mode, set typography to <strong>Ableton Sans</strong>, and align on 8x8 grid.","Save as <code>Production_Compressor.amxd</code>.","Play drum loop in Live: Adjust Threshold and Ratio; confirm transparent dynamic control and active GR metering with zero distortion."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"6. Common Pitfalls and Failure Modes","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Unsmoothed Attack Times Causing Distortion:</strong> Setting Attack time to 0 samples on a 50 Hz bassline. The envelope follower will track the individual wave cycles of the 50 Hz sine wave, modulating gain at 50 Hz and introducing severe harmonic distortion. Always enforce a minimum attack smoothing of 1–5 ms.","<strong>Independent Left/Right Detection Shifting Stereo Image:</strong> Running separate envelope followers on Left and Right channels without linking them. When a loud sound occurs only on the left, the left channel compresses while the right stays loud, skewing the stereo panorama. Always sum or peak-link stereo detection paths.","<strong>Control Rate Zipper Noise:</strong> Modulating Threshold or Makeup Gain using raw message floats without <code>[line~]</code> interpolation."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"7. Self-Assessment & Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Checkpoint Criteria","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"To confirm mastery of this module, the student must successfully complete the following criteria:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Calculate the gain reduction in dB for an input signal peaking at -6 dBFS with a Threshold of -18 dBFS and a Ratio of 4:1.","[ ] Explain why stereo sidechain detection paths must be linked in audio mastering processors.","[ ] Configure <code>[rampsmooth~]</code> to execute a 10 ms attack and 100 ms release at a 48 kHz sample rate."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"8. Source Citations","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Cycling '74. (2024). <em>Max 9 MSP Guide: Dynamics Processing and Envelope Followers</em>. San Francisco: Cycling '74.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Max for Live Production Guidelines: Audio Dynamics Architecture</em>. Ableton Developer Documentation.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Ableton Patch Code Standard: Signal Interpolation and Safety</em>. Ableton Developer Documentation."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"m4l-m-11-2-2","code":"11.2","title":"11.2","blurb":"11.2","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"m4l-l-11-2-1","title":"11.2.1: Audio Craft — Filter and Equalization Topology","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Metadata and Prerequisites","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Lesson ID:</strong> Lesson 11.2.1","<strong>Module / Section:</strong> Module 11 / Section 11.2","<strong>Track:</strong> <code>m4l-devices</code>","<strong>Target Environment:</strong> Ableton Live 12 Standard (with Max for Live add-on) / Live 12 Suite; bundled Cycling '74 Max 9 runtime.","<strong>Operating Systems:</strong> Windows, macOS.","<strong>Prerequisites:</strong> Lesson 11.1.1 - Audio Craft — Dynamics and Gain Processing.","<strong>Primary Sources:</strong>","Cycling '74: <em>Max 9 MSP Guide: Biquad and State-Variable Filter Topologies</em>.","Ableton Production Guidelines: <em>Filter Safety, Ranges, and Exponent Scaling</em>.","Ableton Patch Code Standard: <em>Bypass Topologies and DSP Suppression</em>."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By the conclusion of this module, the student will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["Differentiate between State-Variable Filter (<code>svf~</code>), Biquad Filter (<code>biquad~</code> / <code>filtercoeff~</code>), and Cascade Filter topologies.","Implement logarithmic / exponential parameter scaling on frequency controls to ensure musical response across the 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz spectrum.","Establish safe resonance (Q) bounds to eliminate numerical overflow (<code>NaN</code> / infinity blowup).","Construct a true DSP bypass network that suspends filter calculation when disengaged.","Build a multi-mode parametric EQ band with automatable Frequency, Gain (± 15 dB), and Q controls."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Architectural and Theoretical Foundations","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"3.1 Filter Topology Comparison","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                            FILTER TOPOLOGY TAXONOMY                               |\n+-------------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+\n| Topology          | Objects                       | Primary Characteristics       |\n+-------------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+\n| **State Variable**| `[svf~]`                      | - Simultaneous LP, HP, BP, BR.|\n|                   |                               | - Smooth real-time modulation.|\n|                   |                               | - 12 dB/octave roll-off slope.|\n+-------------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+\n| **Direct Form II**| `[biquad~]` with              | - Supports peaking EQ,        |\n| **Biquad**        | `[filtergraph~]` or           |   shelving, notch, bandpass.  |\n|                   | `[filtercoeff~]`              | - Exact audio-rate coefficient|\n|                   |                               |   control.                    |\n+-------------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+\n| **Cascaded Ladder**| Multi-stage `[svf~]` or       | - 24 dB/octave and 48 dB/oct  |\n|                   | `[lores~]`                    |   steep analog roll-off.      |\n+-------------------+-------------------------------+-------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"heading","text":"3.2 Frequency Parameter Scaling (Logarithmic Response)","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Human hearing perceives pitch and frequency logarithmically. "},{"type":"list","items":["If a frequency control (20 Hz - 20000 Hz) uses linear scaling (1.0), 10000 Hz sits at the center (50%) position. The entire sub-bass, bass, midrange, and treble spectrum (20 Hz - 10000 Hz) is compressed into the first half of the knob.","<strong>The Solution:</strong> In the Inspector for <code>[live.dial]</code>, set <strong>Exponent</strong> to <code>3.0</code> (or <code>3.5</code>). This places 1000 Hz at approximately 50% rotation, distributing musical octaves evenly across the dial arc."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Linear (Exponent = 1.0):\n[ 20 Hz ] ------------------------ [ 10,000 Hz ] ----------------------- [ 20,000 Hz ]\n                (95% of musical pitch spectrum)        (Highest octave only)\n\nLogarithmic (Exponent = 3.3):\n[ 20 Hz ] --- [ 200 Hz ] --- [ 1,000 Hz ] --- [ 5,000 Hz ] --- [ 20,000 Hz ]\n                   (Evenly distributed musical octaves)</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"4. Parametric Biquad Equalization with [filtercoeff~]","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To build a true parametric peaking equalizer:"},{"type":"list","items":["Ingest Frequency (f), Gain (G in dB), and Quality Factor (Q).","Route parameters into <code>[filtercoeff~ @mode peaking]</code>.","<code>filtercoeff~</code> computes five 64-bit biquad coefficients (b_0, b_1, b_2, a_1, a_2) at audio rate.","Route coefficients into <code>[biquad~]</code>."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">        Freq (Hz)         Gain (dB)             Q Factor\n           |                 |                     |\n           v                 v                     v\n    +-----------------------------------------------------+\n    |              filtercoeff~ @mode peaking             |\n    +----+-----------+-----------+-----------+----+-------+\n         |           |           |           |    |\n        (b0)        (b1)        (b2)        (a1) (a2)\n         |           |           |           |    |\n         v           v           v           v    v\n    +-----------------------------------------------------+\n    |                      biquad~                        | &lt;=== [ Audio In ]\n    +--------------------------+--------------------------+\n                               |\n                               v (EQ Processed Audio)</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"5. Laboratory Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Objective","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Construct a professional stereo multi-mode filter featuring Lowpass, Highpass, Bandpass, and Peaking EQ responses with resonance limiting and true DSP bypass."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step-by-Step Procedure","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["In Ableton Live 12, insert a <strong>Max Audio Effect</strong> on an Audio Track and select <strong>Edit in Max</strong>.","Enable <strong>Open in Presentation</strong> in the Patcher Inspector.","Ingest stereo audio from <code>[plugin~]</code>.","Create Filter Controls:","<code>[live.dial]</code> Long Name: <code>Filter_Frequency</code> (Float, <code>20 to 20000 Hz</code>, Exponent: <code>3.3</code>, Initial: <code>1200 Hz</code>).","<code>[live.dial]</code> Long Name: <code>Filter_Resonance</code> (Float, <code>0.1 to 10.0</code>, Exponent: <code>1.5</code>, Initial: <code>0.707</code>).","<code>[live.dial]</code> Long Name: <code>EQ_Gain</code> (Float, <code>-18.0 to +18.0 dB</code>, Initial: <code>0.0 dB</code>).","<code>[live.tab]</code> Long Name: <code>Filter_Mode</code> (Enum: <code>Lowpass Highpass Bandpass Peaking</code>, Initial: <code>0</code>).","Dual-Topology Routing:","For LP/HP/BP: Route Left and Right audio through dual <code>[svf~]</code> objects.","For Peaking EQ: Route through dual <code>[biquad~]</code> objects driven by <code>[filtercoeff~]</code>.","Use <code>[selector~ 4]</code> on Left and Right channels to select the active filter response based on <code>Filter_Mode</code>.","True DSP Bypass:","Create <code>[live.toggle]</code> named <code>Filter_Enable</code>.","When <code>Filter_Enable</code> is <code>0</code>, route dry <code>[plugin~]</code> audio directly to <code>[plugout~]</code> and use <code>[thispoly~]</code> inside a subpatch to halt filter calculation.","Add all controls to Presentation Mode and align on the 8x8 grid.","Save as <code>MultiMode_Filter_Lab.amxd</code>.","In Live, sweep Frequency and Resonance: Confirm pristine, analog-style filter resonance without clipping or numeric blowup."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"6. Common Pitfalls and Failure Modes","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Unbounded Biquad Gain Blowup:</strong> Allowing the Q parameter to reach 100.0 or higher while boosting gain by +18 dB. The filter will generate infinite values (<code>INF</code> / <code>NaN</code>), crashing the audio buffer. Always clamp Q to a maximum of 10.0–18.0 using <code>[clip 0.1 18.0]</code>.","<strong>Frequency Above Nyquist Limit:</strong> Driving filter cutoff frequencies above the Nyquist frequency (f_s / 2, e.g. &gt; 24000 Hz at a 48 kHz sample rate). This causes severe digital aliasing and filter instability. Clamp maximum cutoff to 0.45 × f_s.","<strong>Modulating Un-interpolated Coefficients:</strong> Updating biquad filter coefficients at discrete message rates during high-resonance sweeps, causing audible stepping noise. Use <code>[filtercoeff~]</code> with audio-rate inputs for click-free automation."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"7. Self-Assessment & Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Checkpoint Criteria","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"To confirm mastery of this module, the student must successfully complete the following criteria:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Explain why an Exponent of 3.0 or higher is mandatory on frequency dials spanning 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz.","[ ] Diagram the five coefficient inputs (b_0, b_1, b_2, a_1, a_2) connecting <code>[filtercoeff~]</code> to <code>[biquad~]</code>.","[ ] Demonstrate complete DSP suppression when the filter bypass switch is disengaged."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"8. Source Citations","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Cycling '74. (2024). <em>Max 9 MSP Reference Manual: biquad~, filtercoeff~, and svf~</em>. San Francisco: Cycling '74.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Max for Live Production Guidelines: Filter Topologies and Resonance Bounds</em>. Ableton Developer Documentation.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Ableton Patch Code Standard: Audio Safety &amp; Nyquist Clamping</em>. Ableton Developer Documentation."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"m4l-m-11-3-3","code":"11.3","title":"11.3","blurb":"11.3","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"m4l-l-11-3-1","title":"11.3.1: Audio Craft — Time-Based and Delay Effects","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Metadata and Prerequisites","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Lesson ID:</strong> Lesson 11.3.1","<strong>Module / Section:</strong> Module 11 / Section 11.3","<strong>Track:</strong> <code>m4l-devices</code>","<strong>Target Environment:</strong> Ableton Live 12 Standard (with Max for Live add-on) / Live 12 Suite; bundled Cycling '74 Max 9 runtime.","<strong>Operating Systems:</strong> Windows, macOS.","<strong>Prerequisites:</strong> Lesson 11.2.1 - Audio Craft — Filter and Equalization Topology.","<strong>Primary Sources:</strong>","Cycling '74: <em>Max 9 MSP Guide: Delays, Crossfaders, and Feedback Networks</em>.","Ableton Production Guidelines: <em>Time-Based Devices and Feedback Stability</em>.","Ableton Patch Code Standard: <em>Equal-Power Crossfading and Buffer Clearing</em>."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By the conclusion of this module, the student will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["Build a dual-channel <strong>Stereo Delay Network</strong> supporting both continuous millisecond time and host tempo-synced beat divisions (<code>16n</code>, <code>8nd</code>, <code>4n</code>, <code>2n</code>).","Enforce feedback loop stability using mathematical ceiling limiters (<code>[clip~ -0.95 0.95]</code>) and saturation stages to prevent runaway acoustic oscillation.","Implement an <strong>Equal-Power Dry/Wet Crossfader</strong> using sinusoidal trigonometric panning curves (<code>[cos~]</code> / <code>[sin~]</code>).","Construct a feedback damping filter network to model analog tape and bucket-brigade device (BBD) high-frequency decay.","Implement automatic delay buffer clearing upon transport start to eliminate stale echo tails from previous playback sessions."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Architectural and Theoretical Foundations","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"3.1 Stereo Delay Signal Topology","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"A production delay network processes audio through independent Left/Right delay lines with cross-feedback and damping filters:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                         STEREO DELAY SIGNAL TOPOLOGY                          |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                               |\n|  [ plugin~ ] (Stereo Audio In)                                                |\n|       |                                                                       |\n|       +------------------------------------+                                  |\n|       | (Dry Path)                         | (Wet Delay Path)                 |\n|       |                                    v                                  |\n|       |                           +-------------------+                       |\n|       |                           | tapin~ 3000 (L/R) | &lt;=== [ Feedback In ]  |\n|       |                           +---------+---------+            |          |\n|       |                                     |                      |          |\n|       |                                     v                      |          |\n|       |                           +-------------------+            |          |\n|       |                           | tapout~ (L/R Time)|            |          |\n|       |                           +---------+---------+            |          |\n|       |                                     |                      |          |\n|       |                                     v                      |          |\n|       |                           +-------------------+            |          |\n|       |                           | Damping Filter    |            |          |\n|       |                           | (High-Cut / Tone) |            |          |\n|       |                           +---------+---------+            |          |\n|       |                                     |                      |          |\n|       |                                     v                      |          |\n|       |                           +-------------------+            |          |\n|       |                           | Feedback Limiter  |            |          |\n|       |                           | (Gain *~ &amp; Clip)  |------------+          |\n|       |                           +---------+---------+                       |\n|       |                                     |                                 |\n|       v                                     v (Wet Delayed Audio)             |\n|  +-------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |\n|  |                   Equal-Power Dry/Wet Crossfader                        |  |\n|  +------------------------------------+------------------------------------+  |\n|                                       |                                       |\n|                                       v                                       |\n|                                  [ plugout~ ]                                 |\n|                                                                               |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"heading","text":"3.2 Equal-Power Dry/Wet Crossfading Mathematics","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"A linear crossfader (L_out = Dry × (1 - x) + Wet × x) causes a noticeable -3 dB volume dip at the 50% center mix position. To maintain constant perceived acoustic loudness across the entire mix sweep:"},{"type":"prose","html":"Dry Gain = ( x × 2 ), Wet Gain = ( x × 2 )"},{"type":"prose","html":"Where x [0.0, 1.0] represents the Dry/Wet mix parameter."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">       Mix Parameter x (0.0 to 1.0)\n                   |\n                   v\n       +-----------------------+\n       |   * 0.25 (1/4 Cycle)  |\n       +-----------+-----------+\n                   |\n                   +-------------------+\n                   | (0.0 - 0.25)      |\n                   v                   v\n             +-----------+       +-----------+\n             |   cos~    |       |   sin~    |\n             +-----+-----+       +-----+-----+\n                   | (Dry Scalar)      | (Wet Scalar)\n                   v                   v\n             [ *~ Dry Path ]     [ *~ Wet Path ]</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"4. Feedback Clamping and Buffer Flushing","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"4.1 Feedback Ceiling Limiting","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Runaway feedback can permanently damage hearing and studio monitors."},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Rule:</strong> Multiply feedback by user gain (0.0 - 0.95), and pass through <code>[clip~ -0.99 0.99]</code>.","Optional: Insert a soft-saturation stage (<code>[tanh~]</code>) in the feedback loop to create warm analog compression as repeats build up."],"ordered":false},{"type":"heading","text":"4.2 Delay Buffer Flushing on Transport Reset","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"When a user stops playback in Live and jumps back to Bar 1, old delay repeats in the <code>tapin~</code> memory ring will abruptly play back."},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>The Solution:</strong> Connect <code>[timepoint 1.1.0]</code> → <code>[clear(</code> message into <code>[tapin~]</code>.","When the song restarts at Bar 1, <code>tapin~</code> wipes its internal memory buffer instantly, ensuring a clean start."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"5. Laboratory Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Objective","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Construct a studio-grade stereo delay effect featuring millisecond and tempo-synced time modes, high-cut feedback damping, feedback safety limiting, and an equal-power crossfader."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step-by-Step Procedure","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["In Ableton Live 12, insert a <strong>Max Audio Effect</strong> on an Audio Track and select <strong>Edit in Max</strong>.","Enable <strong>Open in Presentation</strong> in the Patcher Inspector.","Ingest stereo audio from <code>[plugin~]</code>.","Delay Memory Allocation:","Create <code>[tapin~ 4000]</code> for Left and Right channels.","Time Mode Selection:","Create <code>[live.tab Sync_Mode]</code> (Enum: <code>Time (ms)</code> vs <code>Sync (Tempo)</code>).","In Time mode: Send milliseconds from <code>[live.dial Time_L]</code> (1–2000 ms) to <code>[tapout~ 250]</code>.","In Sync mode: Send note divisions (<code>16n</code>, <code>8n</code>, <code>8nd</code>, <code>4n</code>) from <code>[live.menu Sync_L]</code> to <code>[tapout~]</code>.","Feedback &amp; Damping Network:","Route <code>[tapout~]</code> outputs through dual <code>[svf~ 3500. 0.5]</code> (High-Cut Damping).","Route lowpass filtered signal through <code>[live.dial Feedback]</code> (0.0–0.95) → <code>[clip~ -0.95 0.95]</code>.","Route clipped signal back into the inputs of <code>[tapin~ 4000]</code>.","Equal-Power Crossfader:","Create <code>[live.dial Dry_Wet]</code> (0.0 - 100.0%).","Implement the / crossfader network and multiply Dry (<code>plugin~</code>) and Wet (<code>tapout~</code>) signals.","Buffer Flush: Connect <code>[timepoint 1.1.0]</code> to message <code>[clear(</code> → <code>[tapin~]</code>.","Add all controls to Presentation Mode and align on the 8x8 grid.","Save as <code>Production_Stereo_Delay.amxd</code>.","Play in Live: Switch between ms and tempo-synced modes; push feedback to 95%; verify warm analog saturation without audio runaway."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"6. Common Pitfalls and Failure Modes","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Linear Crossfader Volume Loss:</strong> Using linear addition for Dry/Wet mix, creating an audible 3 dB volume drop whenever the mix is centered. Always use equal-power trigonometric curves.","<strong>Infinite Feedback Crash:</strong> Allowing feedback multipliers to reach or exceed <code>1.0</code> without a safety clip object. The delayed signal will amplify exponentially, causing harsh digital noise and freezing the audio thread.","<strong>Pitch Glitching During Tempo Automation:</strong> Automating song tempo while <code>tapout~</code> is set to fixed millisecond values. <code>tapout~</code> must be fed tempo-relative symbols (<code>8n</code>) to track host tempo changes dynamically."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"7. Self-Assessment & Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Checkpoint Criteria","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"To confirm mastery of this module, the student must successfully complete the following criteria:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Diagram the mathematical difference between linear crossfading and equal-power (/) crossfading.","[ ] Configure <code>[tapin~]</code> and <code>[tapout~]</code> to execute a tempo-synced dotted-eighth (<code>8nd</code>) delay.","[ ] Demonstrate automatic memory buffer clearing when Live's transport relocates to the beginning of the timeline."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"8. Source Citations","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Cycling '74. (2024). <em>Max 9 MSP Guide: Delay Lines and Buffer Management</em>. San Francisco: Cycling '74.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Max for Live Production Guidelines: Time-Based Device Architecture</em>. Ableton Developer Documentation.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Ableton Patch Code Standard: Equal-Power Panning &amp; Crossfading</em>. Ableton Developer Documentation."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"m4l-m-11-4-4","code":"11.4","title":"11.4","blurb":"11.4","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"m4l-l-11-4-1","title":"11.4.1: Audio Craft — Nonlinear Saturation and Scopes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Metadata and Prerequisites","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Lesson ID:</strong> Lesson 11.4.1 (Includes Gate H Assessment)","<strong>Module / Section:</strong> Module 11 / Section 11.4","<strong>Track:</strong> <code>m4l-devices</code>","<strong>Target Environment:</strong> Ableton Live 12 Standard (with Max for Live add-on) / Live 12 Suite; bundled Cycling '74 Max 9 runtime.","<strong>Operating Systems:</strong> Windows, macOS.","<strong>Prerequisites:</strong> Lesson 11.3.1 - Audio Craft — Time-Based and Delay Effects.","<strong>Primary Sources:</strong>","Cycling '74: <em>Max 9 MSP Guide: Waveshaping and Nonlinear Functions</em>.","Ableton Production Guidelines: <em>Audio Saturation, Headroom Ceilings, and Scopes</em>.","Ableton Patch Code Standard: <em>UI Thread CPU Management for Visualizers</em>."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By the conclusion of this module, the student will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["Implement nonlinear transfer functions (Hyperbolic Tangent <code>tanh~</code>, Polynomial Saturation, Hard Clipping) to generate musical harmonic distortion.","Build input drive and automatic output makeup / ceiling limiting stages to preserve unity gain.","Integrate <code>[live.scope~]</code> oscilloscope visualizers that dynamically adapt to the device active/disabled state via <code>[live.thisdevice]</code>.","Manage CPU resource allocation between audio-rate DSP computation and low-priority UI rendering.","Complete <strong>Gate H (Audio Craft Mastery)</strong> by authoring and verifying two production-grade audio effects from different functional classes."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Architectural and Theoretical Foundations","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"3.1 Nonlinear Waveshaping Topology","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Nonlinear distortion intentionally alters waveform geometry to introduce harmonic overtones (even/odd harmonics):"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                        NONLINEAR SATURATION TOPOLOGY                          |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                               |\n|  [ plugin~ ] (Audio In)                                                       |\n|       |                                                                       |\n|       v                                                                       |\n|  [ Pre-Drive Stage: *~ (0 to +36 dB) ]                                        |\n|       |                                                                       |\n|       v                                                                       |\n|  +-------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |\n|  | Nonlinear Transfer Function Selector                                    |  |\n|  | - Soft Tape / Tube Saturation:   [tanh~]                                |  |\n|  | - Cubic Polynomial Saturation:   y = x - (x^3 / 3)                      |  |\n|  | - Hard Digital Diode Clipping:   [clip~ -1.0 1.0]                       |  |\n|  +------------------------------------+------------------------------------+  |\n|                                       |                                       |\n|                                       v                                       |\n|  [ Post-Output Ceiling &amp; Auto-Trim ]                                          |\n|       |                                                                       |\n|       +------------------------------------+                                  |\n|       |                                    |                                  |\n|       v                                    v                                  |\n|  [ live.scope~ Visualizer ]           [ plugout~ ] (Saturated Audio Out)      |\n|  (Active state synchronized)                                                  |\n|                                                                               |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"heading","text":"3.2 Mathematical Transfer Functions","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Hyperbolic Tangent (<code>[tanh~]</code>):</strong> Smooth, continuous soft-knee saturation that asymptotically compresses high-amplitude peaks towards ± 1.0, producing rich odd harmonics."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"f(x) = (Drive × x)"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Cubic Polynomial Saturation:</strong> Generates gentle warm saturation for lower drive levels with zero transcendental function overhead."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"f(x) = x - x^33 (for x [-1.0, 1.0])"}]},{"type":"step","title":"4. UI Thread Resource Discipline for live.scope~","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Oscilloscopes and real-time spectrum visualizers can consume significant CPU if misconfigured:"},{"type":"list","items":["Standard <code>live.scope~</code> polling should be throttled to reasonable screen refresh intervals (e.g., 30–60 Hz).","When the device is bypassed in Live, <code>[live.thisdevice]</code> outlet 2 must send <code>active 0</code> to <code>live.scope~</code>, completely halting the visual redraw thread."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"5. Laboratory Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Objective","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Construct an analog-style Saturation and Waveshaping device featuring selectable saturation transfer functions, variable pre-drive, output ceiling limiter, and a theme-compliant <code>live.scope~</code>."},{"type":"heading","text":"Step-by-Step Procedure","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["In Ableton Live 12, insert a <strong>Max Audio Effect</strong> on an Audio Track and select <strong>Edit in Max</strong>.","Enable <strong>Open in Presentation</strong> in the Patcher Inspector.","Ingest stereo audio from <code>[plugin~]</code>.","Pre-Drive Stage:","Create <code>[live.dial Drive]</code> (Float, <code>0.0 to 36.0 dB</code>, Initial: <code>0.0 dB</code>).","Route <code>Drive</code> → <code>[dbtoa]</code> → <code>[1 20(</code>\\rightarrow$ <code>[line~ 1.0]</code>.","Multiply Left and Right audio by the drive signal.","Saturation Stage:","Create <code>[live.tab Sat_Mode]</code> (Enum: <code>Soft (Tape)  Warm (Cubic)  Hard (Clip)</code>).","Mode 1: Route through dual <code>[tanh~]</code>.","Mode 2: Route through cubic polynomial subpatch.","Mode 3: Route through dual <code>[clip~ -1.0 1.0]</code>.","Use <code>[selector~ 3]</code> on Left and Right channels to select the active saturation curve.","Ceiling &amp; Output Trim:","Create <code>[live.dial Output_Ceiling]</code> (Float, <code>-24.0 to 0.0 dB</code>, Initial: <code>-0.5 dB</code>).","Multiply saturated signal by output trim → <code>[clip~ -1.0 1.0]</code> → <code>[plugout~]</code>.","Oscilloscope Integration:","Connect Left saturated output to <code>[live.scope~]</code>.","Route <code>[live.thisdevice]</code> outlet 2 to message <code>[active 1(</code>\\rightarrow$ inlet of <code>[live.scope~]</code>.","Add all parameters to Presentation Mode, set typography to <strong>Ableton Sans</strong>, and align on 8x8 grid.","Save as <code>Production_Saturator_Scope.amxd</code>.","Play bass or drum audio in Live: Drive signal into saturation; observe wave clipping on the oscilloscope; verify that output remains capped below 0 dBFS."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"6. Gate H Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"Note","html":"[!IMPORTANT] <strong>Gate H: Audio Craft Mastery Assessment</strong> To complete Module 11 and verify professional audio DSP competence, the student must complete, test, and archive two distinct audio processors:"},{"type":"heading","text":"Practical Test Assignment:","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Device 1: Multi-Mode Resonant Filter / EQ (<code>MultiMode_Filter_Lab.amxd</code>):</strong>","True State-Variable / Biquad filter topology with continuous, smoothed frequency sweeps.","Exponent 3.0 on frequency dials; safe Q resonance bounds (0.1–18.0).","Full DSP bypass network halting computation when disengaged.","<strong>Device 2: Tempo-Synced Stereo Delay / Saturator (<code>Production_Stereo_Delay.amxd</code> or <code>Production_Saturator_Scope.amxd</code>):</strong>","Equal-power dry/wet crossfade maintaining constant loudness across the sweep.","Feedback safety limiting (&lt; 1.0) with high-frequency damping filter.","Active-state synchronized visual scopes (<code>live.scope~</code> dims on disable).","<strong>Universal Audit:</strong> Both devices must exhibit zero zipper noise during parameter automation, zero DC offset bursts, and clean console silence on load."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"7. Common Pitfalls and Failure Modes","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Unbounded Output Volume from Pre-Drive:</strong> Boosting drive by +36 dB without compensating output gain, blasting downstream tracks and monitor speakers. Always link output trim or provide a brickwall ceiling limiter.","<strong>Aliasing Distortion from Hard Clipping:</strong> Generating harsh, non-harmonic foldover aliasing when clipping high-frequency signals. (For oversampling architectures, see Lesson 17.2.1).","<strong>Leaving Scopes Active When Bypassed:</strong> Neglecting <code>[live.thisdevice]</code> active state wiring, leaving <code>live.scope~</code> redrawing on the UI thread when the device is turned off."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"8. Source Citations","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Cycling '74. (2024). <em>Max 9 MSP Reference Manual: tanh~, clip~, and live.scope~</em>. San Francisco: Cycling '74.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Max for Live Production Guidelines: Audio Saturation and Output Safety</em>, pp. 69–73. Ableton Developer Documentation.","Ableton AG. (2023). <em>Ableton Patch Code Standard: UI Thread Management</em>. Ableton Developer Documentation."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
